ic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "This seminar considers new directions in thinking about political authority that focus on the claims of non-state groups. It considers leading 20th century political theorists who have recognized authority to be plural and contested as well as those who have resisted this characterization. We explore contemporary scholarship about religious groups that claim authority over their members, Indigenous peoples that claim authority over lands and resources, and employers that claim authority over workers by imposing their own rules and norms even if these depart from ones endorsed by the state. The aim is to understand where legitimate authority comes from, how it is enacted, and what role (if any) it plays in shaping the identities of those who are subject to it. \u00a0", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "Co-meets with AMST 6630, GOVT 6835, PHIL 6435, SHUM 6631.", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "Enrollment limited to: 15 students.", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2727, "crseId": 369958, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "SHUM", "catalogNbr": "4632", "titleShort": "Emperors, Kings, Warlords", "titleLong": "Emperors, Kings, and Warlords: Political Legitimacy at the End of the Ancient World", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 16494, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "10:10AM", "timeEnd": "12:05PM", "startDt": "01/22/2019", "endDt": "05/07/2019", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "