scuss how notions of rulership were deployed to challenge emperors and kings. Knowledge of late antique and early medieval political ideas is crucial to understanding modern uses of the so-called \"Western\" past in current political debates.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "Co-meets with\u00a0HIST 6632/MEDVL 6632/SHUM 6632.", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "Enrollment limited to: 15 students.", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2727, "crseId": 369960, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "SHUM", "catalogNbr": "4633", "titleShort": "Following", "titleLong": "Following", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 16504, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "12:20PM", "timeEnd": "02:15PM", "startDt": "01/22/2019", "endDt": "05/07/2019", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "kam468", "firstName": "Kate", "middleName": "A.", "lastName": "Manne"}], "pattern": "T", "facilityDescr": "A D White House 110", "bldgDescr": "A D White House", "facilityDescrshort": "ADW110", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/22/2019", "endDt": "05/07/2019", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "PHIL", "catalogNbr": "4433", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "PHIL", "catalogNbr": "6433", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "SHUM", "catalogNbr": "6633", "type": "B"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/22/2019", "sessionEndDt": "05/07/2019", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "Since Stanley Milgram's famous experiments on obedience to authority conducted in the early 1960s, and arguably long before that, it's been clear that the majority of people are unreliable judges of who to obey, who to follow, and who to treat as moral authority figures. This advanced seminar would begin by considering the nature and bases of a mistaken sense of moral obligation to follow someone's lead, either because one falsely takes oneself to owe them obedience as such, or because one erroneously treats them as a source of superior moral insight. We will then explore questions about the epistemology and metaphysics of genuine or licit moral authority, which is at least partly a matter of issuing, and not contradicting, independently valid moral requirements.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "Co-meets with\u00a0PHIL 6433/SHUM 6633.", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "Enrollment limited to: 15 students.", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2727, "crseId": 369962, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "SHUM", "catalogNbr": "4634", "titleShort": "Curating the British Empire", "titleLong": "Curating the British Empire", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 16510, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "02:30PM", "timeEnd": "04:25PM", "startDt": "01/22/2019", "endDt": "05/07/2019", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "jrr47", "firstName": "Jessica", "middleName": "R.", "lastName": "Ratcliff"}], "pattern": "T", "facilityDescr": "A D White House 109", "bldgDescr": "A D White House", "facilityDescrshort": "ADW109", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/22/2019", "endDt": "05/07/2019", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "C", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "ARTH", "catalogNbr": "4720", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "ARTH", "catalogNbr": "6720", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "BSOC", "catalogNbr": "4634", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "HIST", "catalogNbr": "4634", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "HIST", "catalogNbr": "6634", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "SHUM", "catalogNbr": "6634", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "STS", "catalogNbr": "4634", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "STS", "catalogNbr": "6634", "type": "B"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/22/2019", "sessionEndDt": "05/07/2019", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "During Europe's colonial era, the modern museum emerged as a site of cultural and scientific authority. This course investigates the history of imperial collections and collectors, with a focus on Britain and the East India Company in the nineteenth century. Examples of topics include: the \"supply chain\" for artifacts and knowledge resources; changing conceptions of intellectual property, ownership and access; household versus public versus for-profit collections; museums and the narration of social values and cultural identities; debates over the function or aims of museums and related institutions; the collections and the administration of the empire; the collections and the growth of the sciences; the postcolonial legacies of colonial collections.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "Co-meets with\u00a0ARTH 6720/HIST 6634/SHUM 6634/STS 6634.\u00a0", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "Recommended prerequisite:\u00a0prior coursework in modern history (post-1600).", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "Enrollment limited to: 15 students.", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2727, "crseId": 369964, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "SHUM", "catalogNbr": "4635", "titleShort": "Kafka and Genet", "titleLong": "Authority and Anti-Authority: Kafka and Genet", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 16524, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "12:20PM", "timeEnd": "02:15PM", "startDt": "01/22/2019", "endDt": "05/07/2019", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "ams853", "firstName": "Aaron", "middleName": "M", "lastN